   "answer": "It prints <code>3 3 3</code> for the var loop and <code>0 1 2</code> for the let loop. With <code>var</code> there is one <code>i</code> for the whole loop, and by the time the timers run it is 3. With <code>let</code> each pass of the loop gets its own <code>j</code>.",
   "explain": "<p>Before <code>let</code> existed the fix was an IIFE that passed <code>i</code> as a parameter. Today, use <code>let</code>.</p><p>The timers run after the loop because <code>setTimeout</code> callbacks are tasks, which wait until the current script has finished.</p>",
   "kind": "Output",
   "code": "const out = [];\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => out.push(i));\nfor (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) setTimeout(() => out.push(j));\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(out.join(\" \")));",
