   "answer": "It prints <code>cart</code>, <code>undefined</code> and <code>cart</code>.",
   "explain": "<p><code>cart.regular()</code> is a method call, so <code>this</code> is <code>cart</code>. <code>const f = cart.regular</code> copies the function without the object, and in a module or strict mode a plain call has <code>this</code> as <code>undefined</code>, so <code>this?.name</code> is <code>undefined</code>.</p><p><code>later()</code> uses an arrow function created inside <code>regular</code>, so it keeps the <code>this</code> of that call, which was <code>cart</code>.</p>",
