   "q": "Explain the event loop, microtasks and macrotasks.",
   "hint": "One thread, one call stack, and two queues with different priorities.",
   "answer": "JavaScript runs on one thread with one call stack. When the stack is empty, the event loop first runs every microtask, such as promise callbacks and <code>queueMicrotask</code>, then takes one macrotask, such as a timer or an I/O callback, runs it, and empties the microtask queue again. That is why a resolved promise callback always runs before a <code>setTimeout(fn, 0)</code>.",
   "explain": "<p>The browser renders between tasks, so a long task or an endless chain of microtasks freezes the page. In Node.js, <code>process.nextTick</code> callbacks run even before promise callbacks.</p><p>For testers this explains why awaiting is required: an action that returns a promise has not finished when the next line starts.</p>",
   "points": [
    "Call stack runs to completion",
    "All microtasks run before the next task",
    "Timers and I/O are tasks",
    "Rendering happens between tasks"
   ]
