   "q": "What is the difference between <code>Promise.all</code>, <code>allSettled</code>, <code>race</code> and <code>any</code>?",
   "hint": "Ask for each one: when does it settle, and what happens when one promise fails?",
   "answer": "<code>all</code> resolves with every value when all succeed and rejects as soon as one fails. <code>allSettled</code> always waits for all of them and gives a status and value or reason for each. <code>race</code> settles with the first promise to settle, success or failure. <code>any</code> resolves with the first success and rejects with an <code>AggregateError</code> only if all fail.",
   "explain": "<p>In tests, use <code>all</code> for set up calls that must all work, <code>allSettled</code> to clean up many resources and report every failure, and <code>race</code> to add a timeout. A common Playwright pattern uses <code>Promise.all</code> to start waiting for a response and click at the same time, although <code>const r = page.waitForResponse(...)</code> before the click is clearer.</p>"
