   "hint": "Declarations are set up before the code runs. Which ones get a value right away?",
   "answer": "Before code runs, the engine creates every declared name in its scope. Function declarations are hoisted with their body, so you can call them before their line. <code>var</code> names are hoisted as <code>undefined</code>. <code>let</code>, <code>const</code> and <code>class</code> are hoisted but stay in the temporal dead zone until their line.",
   "explain": "<p>Function expressions and arrow functions assigned to variables follow the rule of the variable, so <code>const f = () =&gt; {}</code> cannot be called before its line.</p>"
