   "q": "What causes memory leaks in JavaScript, and how would you find one?",
   "hint": "The garbage collector frees what can no longer be reached. What keeps things reachable by mistake?",
   "answer": "Common causes are global variables, timers and intervals that are never cleared, event listeners that are never removed, caches and maps that only grow, closures that hold large objects, and DOM nodes removed from the page but still referenced in JavaScript. Find them with heap snapshots in Chrome DevTools or <code>node --inspect</code>, comparing snapshots taken before and after repeating an action.",
   "explain": "<p>In long test runs, a leak in the test code itself, such as a listener added in every <code>beforeEach</code>, can slow the suite down until it times out. <code>WeakMap</code>, <code>WeakRef</code> and <code>AbortController</code> for removing listeners help avoid leaks.</p>",
