   "answer": "CommonJS uses <code>require</code> and <code>module.exports</code>, loads files synchronously and is Node.js only. ES modules use <code>import</code> and <code>export</code>, are the standard for browsers and Node.js, are resolved before the code runs, support top level <code>await</code> and always run in strict mode. Exports in ES modules are live bindings, not copies.",
   "explain": "<p>In Node.js, a file is an ES module when it ends in <code>.mjs</code> or the nearest package.json has <code>\"type\": \"module\"</code>. Modern Node.js can also <code>require()</code> an ES module that has no top level await.</p><p>In ES modules there is no <code>__dirname</code>. Use <code>import.meta.dirname</code> instead.</p>"
