   "lv": "m",
   "q": "If Node.js is single threaded, how does it handle many requests at once?",
   "hint": "Who does the waiting while JavaScript keeps running?",
   "answer": "Your JavaScript runs on one thread, but slow operations such as network and file access are handed to the operating system or to the libuv thread pool. While they run, the event loop keeps serving other requests, and the callbacks run on the main thread when the results arrive. CPU heavy work blocks everything, so it belongs in <code>worker_threads</code> or a separate process.",
   "explain": "<p>This is why Node.js suits I/O heavy servers and test tools that drive browsers and APIs, and why a synchronous loop that runs for two seconds stops every other request for those two seconds.</p>"
